"au:Fu_X in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AFu_X+in%3Amath)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; @misc{2608.15643, author = {Xiao-Ye Fu and Yue-Kun Li and Wei-Jie Wang and Xuan Wang}, title = {{T}urán problem on the union of three intervals of equal length}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2608.15643}, note = {arXiv:2608.15643v2} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... This paper addresses the Turán extremal problem on the symmetric three-interval set $\\Omega_\\lambda = (-1,1) \\cup (\\lambda-1,\\lambda+1) \\cup (-\\lambda-1,-\\lambda+1)$, $\\lambda \\geq 0$.\n\n  * Turán problem on the union of three intervals of equal length\n\nXiao-Ye Fu, Yue-Kun Li, Wei-Jie Wang, Xuan Wang\n\nAug 18 2026 math.CA math.FA arXiv:2608.15643v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2608.15643, author = {Xiao-Ye Fu and Yue-Kun Li and Wei-Jie Wang and Xuan Wang}, title = {{T}urán problem on the union of three intervals of equal length}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2608.15643}, note = {arXiv:2608.15643v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nThis paper addresses the Turán extremal problem on the symmetric three-interval set $\\Omega_\\lambda = (-1,1) \\cup (\\lambda-1,\\lambda+1) \\cup (-\\lambda-1,-\\lambda+1)$, $\\lambda \\geq 0$. We establish a discrete approximation principle relating the continuous Turán problem on $\\Omega_\\lambda$ to the discrete Turán problem on an associated finite discrete set. This enables the continuous problem to be exactly expressed as a limit of finite nonnegative trigonometric polynomial problems, which are equivalent to finite semidefinite programs. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn finding maximum-cardinality symmetric subsets - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0925772102000469)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; In this paper we study the complexity of the problem of finding a symmetric subset of maximum cardinality among \\text{n} points in the plane, or in three-dimensional space, and some related problems like the largest repetitive or \\text{k}-repetitive subsets. For the maximum-cardinality symmetric subset problem in the plane we obtain an algorithm of complexity .\n\n## Computational Geometry\n\nDate: January 2003\n\nPages: 19-25\n\nVolume: Volume 24, Issue 1\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# On finding maximum-cardinality symmetric subsets☆\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Peter Brass ^{1}\n\nShow more\n\nView PDF Download full issue\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/S0925-7721(02)00046-9\n\nMore actions\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this paper we study the complexity of the problem of finding a symmetric subset of maximum cardinality among \\text{n} points in the plane, or in three-dimensional space, and some related problems like the largest repetitive or \\text{k}-repetitive subsets. For the maximum-cardinality symmetric subset problem in the plane we obtain an algorithm of complexity .\n\n## Keywords\n\nPoint patterns\n\n;\n\nSymmetry detection\n\n;\n\nSymmetric subsets\n\n;\n\nRegular polygons\n\n\nA preliminary version of this paper appeared in the JCDCG 2000 proceedings, Springer LNCS 2098 (2001) 106–112.\n\n^{1}\n    \n\nSupported by the DFG, Heisenberg grant BR 1465/5-2.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGreg Martin: Paper Abstract (https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/index.shtml?abstract=SSPCRT)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week;   * Download the paper The symmetric subset problem in continuous Ramsey theory in PDF format (official Experiment.\n\n#### The symmetric subset problem in continuous Ramsey theory\n\nwith Kevin O'Bryant A symmetric subset of the reals is one that remains invariant under some reflection z → c-z. Given 0 < ε ≤ 1, there exists a real number Δ(ε) with the following property: if 0 ≤ δ < Δ(ε), then every subset of [0,1] with measure ε contains a symmetric subset with measure δ, while if δ > Δ(ε), then there exists a subset of [0,1] with measure ε that does not contain a symmetric subset with measure δ. In this paper we establish upper and lower bounds for Δ(ε) of the same order of magnitude: for example, we prove that Δ(ε) = 2ε - 1 for 11/16 ≤ ε ≤ 1 and that 0.59ε^{2} < Δ(ε) < 0.8ε^{2} for 0 < ε ≤ 11/16.This continuous problem is intimately connected with a corresponding discrete problem. A set S of integers is called a B^{*}[g] set if for any given m, there are at most g ordered pairs (s_{1},s_{2}) ∈ S×S with s_{1} + s_{2} = m; in the case g = 2, these are better known as Sidon sets. Our lower bound on Δ(ε) implies that every B^{*}[g] set contained in {1, 2, ..., n} has cardinality less than 1.30036 √{gn}. This improves a result of Green for g ≥ 30. Conversely, we use a probabilistic construction of B^{*}[g] sets to establish an upper bound on Δ(ε) for small ε.Two of our other joint papers, Constructions of generalized Sidon sets and The supremum of autoconvolutions, with applications to additive number theory, are closely related.\n\n  * Download the paper The symmetric subset problem in continuous Ramsey theory in PDF format (official Experiment. Math. version)\n  * See the Math Review for this paper (restricted to MathSciNet subscribers): HTML or PDF format--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Experimental Mathematics, Volume 16 (https://dblp.org/db/journals/em/em16.html)\nciteturn3search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; The Symmetric Subset Problem in Continuous Ramsey Theory.\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/em/MartinO07\n\nGreg Martin, Kevin O'Bryant:\nThe Symmetric Subset Problem in Continuous Ramsey Theory. 145-165\n  * Image: Journal Articles\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI\n      * unpaywalled version\n 